
{"id":"d9efa8d9-8b94-424e-9a3e-301fe3a9635a","object":"chat.completion","created":1785036811,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\\n    id2node = {node['id']: node for node in nodes}\\n    retained = set()\\n    # propagate a node and all its ancestors into retained\\n    def mark_ancestors(node_id):\\n        while node_id is not None and node_id in id2node:\\n            retained.add(node_id)\\n            node_id = id2node[node_id]['parent']\\n    # start with root\\n    root_id = next(node['id'] for node in nodes if node['parent'] is None)\\n    retained.add(root_id)\\n    # mark all non-removable nodes and their ancestors\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            mark_ancestors(node['id'])\\n    # cover required symbols\\n    covered = set()\\n    for nid in retained:\\n        covered.add(id2node[nid]['symbol'])\\n    for sym in required_symbols:\\n        if sym in covered:\\n            continue\\n        # find candidates with this symbol not already retained\\n        candidates = [node for node in nodes if node['symbol'] == sym and node['id'] not in retained]\\n        if not candidates:\\n            continue\\n        # choose the one with highest utility\\n        best = max(candidates, key=lambda n: n['utility'])\\n        mark_ancestors(best['id'])\\n        # update covered symbols\\n        for nid in retained:\\n            covered.add(id2node[nid]['symbol'])\\n    removed_ids = [node['id'] for node in nodes if node['id'] not in retained]\\n    retained_ids = list(retained)\\n    return {'removed_node_ids': removed_ids, 'retained_node_ids': retained_ids}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to understand the task. We are given a case payload with a tree structure of nodes, each having id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also required_symbols is a list of symbols that must be retained (some nodes may have those symbols). The task likely is: given a tree of nodes, determine which nodes to remove and which to retain, subject to some constraints. The output schema requires \"removed_node_ids\" and \"retained_node_ids\" as arrays of strings.\n\nThe problem is to figure out the algorithm. The fixture shows a tree with root (not removable), and children. Required_symbols: [\"root\", \"s1\"]. So we must keep nodes that have symbols \"root\" and \"s1\", but also the tree structure? Possibly we need to prune removable nodes that are not necessary for required symbols? Or we need to maximize utility? The given nodes have utility values. Possibly the goal is to remove removable nodes (those with removable: true) that are not needed to cover required symbols? But we must also consider that removing a node might affect its descendants? Since it's a tree, if we remove a node, its descendants are also gone? The problem statement is not explicitly given: \"Registered procedure task SE-PE-01\". We need to infer from the examples and the fact that we have utilities and removable flags. Possibly we need to select a subset of nodes to retain such that all required symbols are present, and we want to maximize total utility of retained nodes? Or minimize removed utility? Since we output both removed and retained, it's a partition.\n\nLet's examine the first case payload (fg1-se-pe-01-b-000). Nodes:\n- root: id root, parent null, symbol root, depth0, removable false, utility 1.0\n- n1: parent root, symbol s1, depth1, removable false, utility 0.339\n- n2: parent root, symbol s2, depth1, removable true, utility 0.798\n- n3: parent root, symbol s0, depth1, removable false, utility 0.124\n- n4: parent n2, symbol s1, depth2, removable true, utility 0.428\n- n5: parent n3, symbol s2, depth2, removable false, utility 0.428\n- n6: parent n1, symbol s0, depth2, removable true, utility 0.721\n\nRequired symbols: root and s1. \n\nWe need to decide which nodes to remove and retain. The root is not removable, so retained. n1 has symbol s1, required, and removable is false, so retained. n2 has symbol s2, not required, removable true -> possibly removable? n3 has s0, not required, removable false -> must retain? n4 has s1 (required) but removable true, and its parent n2 is removable? n5 has s2, not required, removable false, retains. n6 has s0, not required, removable true.\n\nIf we remove n2, then n4 (its child) might be affected? In tree terms, if a parent is removed, the child is also effectively removed? Typically in pruning, if you remove a node, its subtree is also removed. But the input is flat nodes with parent references. We need to decide which nodes to include in the output. The output is removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids, meaning we choose a subset of nodes to retain, and the rest are considered removed. But the tree structure implies that retention must respect ancestry? Possibly we cannot retain a node if its parent is removed. So retained set must form a connected subtree from root? Possibly the rule is: we must retain all non-removable nodes? But some non-removable nodes have parent that is removable? Actually, removable flag is on the node itself, not on the parent. The root is not removable. n1 is not removable. n3 is not removable. n5 is not removable. So these must be retained. n2, n4, n6 are removable. But if we remove n2, can we still retain n4? n4's parent is n2. If n2 is removed, n4 cannot be retained because its ancestor is gone. So n4 would also be implicitly removed. Similarly, n6's parent is n1 (non-removable), so n6 can be independently removed or retained? But n6 is removable, so we can decide.\n\nGoal: retain required symbols. Required: root (retained), s1. Which nodes have symbol s1? n1 (non-removable) and n4 (removable, descendant of n2). So s1 is already satisfied by n1. So we don't need n4. So we can remove n4. n2 is not required, can remove. n6 is not required, can remove. n3 and n5 are non-removable, so they stay? n5 is non-removable, its parent n3 is non-removable, so both are retained. n6's parent n1 is retained, but n6 is removable and not required, so we can remove n6.\n\nThus removed_node_ids: n2, n4, n6? But n2 is removable, n4 removable, n6 removable. What about any other? All non-removable are retained. So retained: root, n1, n3, n5. That seems plausible.\n\nNow check second case (b-001): similar structure but different utilities. Nodes: root, n1 symbol s1 removable false, n2 removable true, n3 removable false, n4 removable true, n5 removable false, n6 removable true. Same tree structure. Required symbols root and s1. So same reasoning: retain root, n1, n3, n5; remove n2, n4, n6. So output should be consistent across these cases if the algorithm is deterministic: retain all non-removable nodes, and also maybe some removable nodes if needed for required symbols? But here required symbols are already covered by non-removable n1, so no removable needed. So we remove all removable nodes. That seems too simple.\n\nBut consider if required symbol s1 only appeared in a removable node (e.g., n4) and not in any non-removable? In these examples, n1 is non-removable and has s1, so it's covered. But what if there was no non-removable node with s1? Then we might need to retain a removable node that provides s1, even though its parent might be removable? But we must retain the parent chain to keep the node. So more complex.\n\nThe task might be: \"Prune the tree to satisfy required symbols, maximizing utility of retained nodes.\" Since utilities are given, that would be a typical problem: select a subset of nodes to retain that includes all required symbols and forms a connected subtree from root (since root is always retained? Actually root is not removable, but its descendants can be removed). Retained nodes must form a valid tree (every node's parent must also be retained). The objective is to maximize sum utility 