ust be retained (and its ancestors must be retained too, even if they are removable). So we need to propagate.\n\nThus the task is: Given a tree with nodes that have removable boolean, and a list of required symbols, determine which nodes to retain such that:\n- Root is always retained (it is non-removable? In fixtures root has removable false.)\n- Each required symbol must appear in at least one retained node.\n- If a node is retained, all its ancestors must be retained.\n- Non-removable nodes must be retained (if their ancestors are retained? But ancestors could be removable? If a non-removable node's parent is removable, we cannot remove the parent because then the non-removable child would be orphaned. So we must retain the parent. Thus the rule: if a node is non-removable, its entire ancestor chain must be retained. So effectively, we must retain all non-removable nodes and all their ancestors. That ensures root is retained.\n- For removable nodes, we can choose to remove them, but if removing a removable node would cause a required symbol to be missing (because all nodes with that symbol are descendants of that removable node), then we must retain that removable node (and its ancestors). Similarly, if a removable node is an ancestor of a required symbol node that is retained (e.g., a non-removable node with required symbol), then it must be retained anyway because the non-removable descendant forces it. So actually, the only removable nodes that can be removed are those that are not on the path from root to any non-removable node, and not on the path to any required symbol that is only available in removable nodes (which we might need to retain). Additionally, if a required symbol appears in a non-removable node, then all its ancestors are already retained due to non-removable propagation. So the only additional required symbols that might force retention of removable nodes are those that do not appear in any non-removable node. In that case, we must find the 